{"name":"Briar Rose","hex":"#c07281","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"briar","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of many plants with thorny stems growing in dense clusters, such as many in the Rosa, Rubus, and Smilax genera.","Anything that is sharp or unpleasant to the feelings."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Tree heath (Erica arborea), a thorny Mediterranean shrub.","A pipe for smoking, made from the roots of that shrub."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/briar"},{"term":"rose","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shrub of the genus Rosa, with red, pink, white or yellow flowers.","A flower of the rose plant."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make rose-colored; to redden or flush.","To perfume, as with roses."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rose"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sleeping Beauty","blocks":[{"text":"\"Sleeping Beauty\", also titled in English as The Sleeping Beauty in the Woods, is a fairy tale about a princess cursed by an evil fairy to sleep for a hundred years before being awakened by a handsome prince. A good fairy, knowing the princess would be frightened if alone when she wakes, uses her wand to put every living person and animal in the palace and forest asleep, to awaken when the princess does."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Sleeping_Beauty_by_Harbour.jpg/330px-Sleeping_Beauty_by_Harbour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sleeping_Beauty","wikidata":"Q11824"},{"title":"Aurora (Sleeping Beauty)","blocks":[{"text":"Aurora, also known as Sleeping Beauty or Briar Rose, is a fictional character who appears in Disney Animation's 1959 film Sleeping Beauty. Voiced by Mary Costa, Aurora is the only child of King Stefan and Queen Leah. An evil fairy named Maleficent seeks revenge for not being invited to Aurora's christening and curses the newborn princess, foretelling that she will prick her finger on a spinning wheel's spindle and die before sunset on her sixteenth birthday. Merryweather, one of the three good fairies, weakened the curse so Aurora would only sleep. Determined to prevent this, three good fairies raise Aurora as a peasant in order to protect her, patiently awaiting her sixteenth birthday—the day the spell can only be broken by a kiss from her true love, Prince Phillip."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/7f/Aurora_%28Sleeping_Beauty%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aurora_(Sleeping_Beauty)","wikidata":"Q519034"},{"title":"Briar Rose (novel)","blocks":[{"text":"Briar Rose is a young adult novel written by American author Jane Yolen, published in 1992. Incorporating elements of Sleeping Beauty, it was published as part of the Fairy Tale Series of novels compiled by Terri Windling. The novel won the annual Mythopoeic Fantasy Award for Adult Literature in 1993. It was also nominated for the Nebula Award for Best Novel."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/53/Briar_Rose_%281992%29-Jane_Yolen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Briar_Rose_(novel)","wikidata":"Q4965778"},{"title":"Rose","blocks":[{"text":"A rose is either a woody perennial flowering plant of the genus Rosa, in the family Rosaceae, or the flower it bears. There are over three hundred species and tens of thousands of cultivars. The plants range in size and form, from trailing and erect shrubs to climbers up to 7 metres (23 ft) in height. Sharp prickles appear on the stems of many species. Their flowers vary in size and shape and are usually large and showy, in colours ranging from white through pinks, reds, oranges and yellows."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Rosa_rubiginosa_1.jpg/330px-Rosa_rubiginosa_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rose","wikidata":"Q34687"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Ring34.jpg/500px-Ring34.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Ring34.jpg/500px-Ring34.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An older image of the sleeping princess: Brünnhilde, surrounded by magical fire rather than roses (illustration by Arthur Rackham to Richard Wagner's Die Walküre)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARing34.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Henry_Meynell_Rheam_-_Sleeping_Beauty.jpg/500px-Henry_Meynell_Rheam_-_Sleeping_Beauty.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Henry_Meynell_Rheam_-_Sleeping_Beauty.jpg/500px-Henry_Meynell_Rheam_-_Sleeping_Beauty.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sleeping Beauty, by Henry Meynell Rheam, 1899","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHenry_Meynell_Rheam_-_Sleeping_Beauty.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Dornr%C3%B6schen.jpg/500px-Dornr%C3%B6schen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Dornr%C3%B6schen.jpg/1280px-Dornr%C3%B6schen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Dornr%C3%B6schen.jpg/500px-Dornr%C3%B6schen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sleeping Beauty is shown a spindle by the old woman. Sleeping Beauty, by Alexander Zick (1845–1907)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.or